GLOBALS (do not re-declare): showPopup(title,body), showTooltip(text,event), hideTooltip(), #container (mount target), window.__markRendered() (call once when drawn), 'fig-resize' CustomEvent on document.

PLOTTING HELPERS (SVG.js track; do not re-declare, and do NOT define your own X()/Y() — use plotX/plotY):
  setPlotFrame({ xDomain, yDomain, margin }) → call ONCE before creating plots; establishes world→screen and owns resize (do not write your own plot resize handler)
  plotX(worldX) / plotY(worldY) → screen px
  compileExpr(src, vars) → f(...args); throws on a bad expression and never returns a fallback
  bindEquationInput(inputId, targets, { variables, onChange }) → wires an <input> to plot handles and shows parse errors inline
  plotFunction(id, { expr, variables, domain, samples, stroke, width, dashed }) → handle
  fillBetween(id, { lower, upper, variables, domain, samples, fill, opacity }) → handle
  plotParametric(id, { x, y, variables, tDomain, samples, stroke }) → handle
  variables: the argument names the expression may use, in order. Defaults are plotFunction ['x'],
  fillBetween ['x'], plotParametric ['t']. When a blueprint field declares "variables", you MUST pass
  that same array to BOTH the plot constructor and bindEquationInput — a field with variables ["t"]
  plotted via plotFunction('f', { expr: 't^2' }) without { variables: ['t'] } throws
  "Unknown variable t" at construction and kills the whole figure.
  integrateSimpson(fn, a, b, n) → number;  findRoot(fn, a, b, tol) → number | null
  Plot handles re-sample on EVERY setter call. Setters differ by helper:
    plotFunction   → .setExpr(src) / .setFn(f) / .setDomain([a,b])
    fillBetween    → .setLower(src) / .setUpper(src) / .setDomain([a,b])
    plotParametric → .setX(src) / .setY(src)
  All handles also have .sample() and .remove().

LIBRARY: SVG.js v3 (global: SVG). var draw=SVG().addTo(document.getElementById('container')).size('100%','100%'); var W=600,H=Math.round(W*(3/4)); draw.viewbox(0,0,W,H). font-size in user units (no px). Hover: el.on('mousemove',e=>showTooltip('label',e)).on('mouseleave',()=>hideTooltip()).on('click',()=>showPopup('Title','body')); el.css('cursor','pointer'). window.__markRendered() synchronously at end.

VISUAL FIDELITY: the default state must be a faithful inline replacement for the source image.
- Color scheme: match every color exactly — axis lines, data series, fills, backgrounds
- Layout & proportions: same relative element positions, same axis ranges, same tick marks and gridlines
- Data shape: match curve trajectories, bar heights, scatter clusters, or node/edge layout — approximate magnitudes, not invented
- Labels & annotations: every label visible in the original must appear at the same position; use blueprint.reconstructionNotes for text and color coding
- Line styles: dashed vs solid, stroke weights, arrowhead presence — copy from the image
- The step-0 (default) state should look indistinguishable from the original at a glance — a student replacing a static figure with this widget should see the same image they expect
- No title bar or toolbar visible by default

TEXT PLACEMENT & READABILITY: faithful labels must still be readable.
- Treat every label, tick label, annotation, formula, and legend item as a collision-checked object with a real text box.
- Before finalizing, check each text box against lines, arrows, curves, points, nodes, tick marks, gridlines, controls, and other text. Text must not sit on top of strokes or dense marks.
- If the source label is visually anchored to a busy line/arrow/node, keep the semantic anchor but move the text to the nearest open whitespace and connect it with a short leader line, pointer, or subtle dot if needed.
- Draw text and label backgrounds after geometry so labels are on top. For labels over busy regions, add a small white or translucent background rectangle, or use SVG paint-order/stroke halo: text.attr({ 'paint-order':'stroke', stroke:'#fff', 'stroke-width':3, 'stroke-linejoin':'round' }).
- Do not add extra visible explanatory text inside the figure; use hover tooltips, click popups, and the scaffold narration panel for explanation.

SCALE SAFETY: figures may be stitched into chapters at different sizes.
- If using Canvas, Chart.js custom drawing, or any pixel-based renderer, derive point radius, stroke width, arrowheads, ticks, label size, and annotation padding from min(plotWidth, plotHeight).
- Avoid large fixed pixel minimums such as Math.max(20, scaledRadius); use only small clamps that prevent invisibility.
- If using SVG/viewBox with naturally scaling geometry, preserve that behavior and avoid fixed px sizes that break scaling.
- If space is small, simplify or hide secondary labels instead of making marks oversized.

HOVER/CLICK (on user action only): hover → showTooltip(label,event)/hideTooltip(); cursor:pointer on interactive elements. Click → showPopup('Title','2–3 sentence explanation'). Animation: fill/opacity transitions only; no scale/translate/bounce.

CONTROLS (blueprint.interactions — render in UI section; add id="{id}Input" to every input):
  slider → <label>{label}: <output id="{id}Val">{default}</output><input id="{id}Input" type="range" min="{min}" max="{max}" step="{step}" value="{default}" oninput="document.getElementById('{id}Val').value=this.value;update_{id}(+this.value)"></label>  +  function update_{id}(v){ /* teach: blueprint.interactions[].teaches */ }
  toggle → <label><input id="{id}Input" type="checkbox" onchange="toggle_{id}(this.checked)"> {label}</label>  +  function toggle_{id}(on){}
  button → <button onclick="trigger_{id}()">{label}</button>  +  function trigger_{id}(){}
  equation_input → one <label>{field.label} <input id="{field.id}Input" value="{field.default}"></label> per entry in the interaction's "fields"
Each control's handler must teach the concept described in blueprint.interactions[].teaches.

EQUATION INPUTS (when any interaction has type "equation_input") — the scaffold owns parsing and re-sampling; do not hand-roll either:
  1. Call setPlotFrame({ xDomain, yDomain, margin }) first, then use plotX / plotY for every coordinate.
  2. Create a plot handle per field with plotFunction / fillBetween / plotParametric, using the field's
     "domain" AND its "variables" (e.g. plotFunction(id, { expr: field.default, variables: field.variables,
     domain: field.domain })) — the constructor compiles the expression immediately and throws on an
     undeclared variable.
  3. Wire each input with bindEquationInput('{field.id}Input', handle, { variables: field.variables }) — this
     bare-handle form only works for plotFunction (bindEquationInput defaults to calling .setFn on the target,
     and only plotFunction has that method). For a fillBetween boundary pass { handle, method: 'setLower' } or
     { handle, method: 'setUpper' }; for a plotParametric component pass { handle, method: 'setX' } or
     { handle, method: 'setY' } — fillBetween and plotParametric have no .setFn/.setExpr and a bare handle
     would throw.
  4. NEVER hardcode the source figure's topology and re-solve only a few corner/scalar values. If the
     source shows a straight-edged region, the drawn path must STILL come from sampling the compiled function,
     so typing a curved expression produces a curved result. A polygon built from a fixed list
     of corner points is a failure even when those corners are recomputed.
  5. NEVER wrap evaluation in a try/catch that silently restores a default expression or default geometry.
     bindEquationInput already keeps the last good curve and shows the parse error inline; adding your own
     fallback is what makes edits appear to do nothing.
  6. Recompute every derived answer the figure reports (e.g. an integral value) with integrateSimpson over
     the same compiled function, so the number and the picture can never disagree.

DEMO STEPS (blueprint.demo_steps): if present and non-empty, build a step player in #ui:
  - A <p id="demoNarration" style="margin:0;font-size:10px;max-width:180px"> showing the current step's narration
  - <button onclick="prevStep()">◀</button> <span id="stepLabel"></span> <button onclick="nextStep()">▶</button>
  - Each step's .state sets matching interaction inputs to the specified values and calls their update functions
  - Step 0 is the default view (set on load); subsequent steps are one click away
  - Omit the player entirely if demo_steps is absent or empty

userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

FAITHFULNESS FIRST: the default state must be a pixel-accurate inline replacement for the source image — same colors, same data shape, same labels, same proportions. A student who sees the static figure in the textbook should recognize it immediately.

THEN ADD INTERACTIVITY: implement any controls from blueprint.interactions so a student can explore the concept hands-on. Each control's handler should teach the relationship described in its "teaches" field. Use hover tooltips and click popups to surface explanations on demand.

CONCEPT: Decomposing double integrals into sub-regions when boundary formulas change, and avoiding decomposition by changing the order of integration.
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): If the boundary of an integration region changes analytical formula mid-way, you must split the region into separate double integrals. Choosing the alternative integration order (Type II instead of Type I) often avoids this split entirely.
DEMO STEPS: 3 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "Decomposing double integrals into sub-regions when boundary formulas change, and avoiding decomposition by changing the order of integration.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "x-axis",
    "y-axis",
    "line y=3",
    "line y=-x+1",
    "line y=x+1",
    "region R1 (blue shaded area, left)",
    "region R2 (yellow shaded area, right)",
    "vertical strip in R1 (red vertical segment)",
    "vertical strip in R2 (red vertical segment)",
    "horizontal strip (when Type II is active)",
    "labels: y = 3, y = -x + 1, y = x + 1, R1, R2, 0, -2, 2, x, y",
    "integral math display showing integral limits"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "integrationType",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "how changing the slice orientation (Type I vs Type II) can reduce two integrals into a single, simpler integral",
      "label": "Integration Direction",
      "options": [
        "Type I (Vertical Slices)",
        "Type II (Horizontal Slices)"
      ],
      "default": "Type I (Vertical Slices)"
    },
    {
      "id": "leftBoundary",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "teaches": "how the formula of the left lower boundary determines the shape and integration limits of R1",
      "label": "Left Lower Boundary",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "y_left",
          "label": "y_left(x) =",
          "default": "-x + 1",
          "variables": [
            "x"
          ],
          "domain": [
            -3,
            0
          ],
          "role": "boundary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "rightBoundary",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "teaches": "how the formula of the right lower boundary determines the shape and integration limits of R2",
      "label": "Right Lower Boundary",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "y_right",
          "label": "y_right(x) =",
          "default": "x + 1",
          "variables": [
            "x"
          ],
          "domain": [
            0,
            3
          ],
          "role": "boundary"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Why Split the Region?",
      "narration": "In Type I integration, we slice vertically. Looking from left to right, the lower boundary changes its formula at x = 0 from y = -x + 1 to y = x + 1. This forces us to split the region into R1 and R2.",
      "state": {
        "integrationType": "Type I (Vertical Slices)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Modify the Boundaries",
      "narration": "Try editing the boundary equations. Notice how the blue (R1) and yellow (R2) regions automatically adjust their shapes and limits based on your functions.",
      "state": {
        "integrationType": "Type I (Vertical Slices)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The Type II Shortcut",
      "narration": "Switch to Type II (horizontal) integration. By slicing horizontally, the left boundary is always x = -(y - 1) and the right boundary is always x = y - 1, meaning we only need a single integral!",
      "state": {
        "integrationType": "Type II (Horizontal Slices)"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "If the boundary of an integration region changes analytical formula mid-way, you must split the region into separate double integrals. Choosing the alternative integration order (Type II instead of Type I) often avoids this split entirely.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Create an interactive 2D Cartesian coordinate plane. Show a triangular region bounded by y=3 at the top. The lower left boundary is colored blue, lower right is colored orange. Left subregion R1 is shaded pale blue with a red vertical strip; right subregion R2 is shaded pale yellow with a red vertical strip. When Type II is selected, the vertical split disappears, the entire region merges into one color (pale purple), and a single blue horizontal strip with arrows pointing left/right is shown instead. Render dynamic LaTeX equations below the graph showing the resulting integral setups."
}
